One track mind

Ernest Obeng never ceases to amaze. You would think that what with nearing the end of his research work for a Ph.D at Loughborough University as well as scorching boards and tracks around Europe he would have little time left for other diversions. Not so. In his quieter moments Ernest is a poet of no mean ability. A collection of his work has recently been published by the Exposition Press of Florida under the appropriate title 'One track mind'. Not unexpectedly the subject matter is athletic endeavour with 45 poems illustrated by guess who? Right! If ever you wondered what it was like to sprint at the highest level Ernest's poem 'Just 10' will give you an idea.

To you it's just ten seconds;
To me it's been five years.
To you it's just a 100 dash;
To me it's sweat and tears.

To you it's just the gun then run;
To me it's skills, technique.
To you it's raw aggression, strength;
To me it's power and peak.

To you it's just dive for tape;
To me it's body lean.
To you it's over far too soon;
To me it's long and mean.

To you these men of varied sizes
And shapes crouched on one knee
Might just as well be saying prayers
But who says prayers for me?

The success of his book has surprised Ernest and he has even had a request from a blind person to allow it to be reproduced in braille. If you would like to see it for yourself copies at £3.50 (p&p inc) may be obtained from [address withheld].

Race Walking.

BELGRAVIAN WALKING SECTION REPORT by Carl Lawton

The winter did not prove to be all that successful, we have managed to survive with a reduced turn out and have sometimes been down to a bare team or even no team at all. I would like to think the summer will be different. As always we seem to have to rely on the 'old faithfuls' and little coming through to help them out. But all is not gloom and doom we have some young faces on the horizon.

